#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Robert Briffault|chapter=The Great Mothers|title=The Mothers: A Study of the Origins of Sentiments and Institutions|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Macmillan Publishers (United States)|The Macmillan Company]]|year=1927|volume=III|pages=146–147|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KoEcAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA147|oclc=530511|passage=[T]hose deities which are called ‘chthonic’ or earthly are, as a matter of fact, not in any sense deities of the earth. They are, in one of their aspects associated with the ‘underworld,’ but that underworld is but a segment of their cycle; they are deities of the underworld not because they appertain to the earth or are in any sense a personification of it, but because they are, on the contrary, heavenly bodies which, in the course of their cycle, pass under the earth. They are therefore just as much ‘'''uranian'''’ as are the Olympians.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Mircea Eliade|chapter=The ‘God who Binds’ and the Symbolism of Knots|translator=w:Philip Mairet|title=Images and Symbols: Studies in Religious Symbolism|edition=Mythos paperback|location=Princeton, N.J.|publisher=w:Princeton University Press|year=1991|pages=96–97|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=oH_D4hwXDJEC&pg=PA97|isbn=978-0-691-02068-6|passage=If he [the Hindu god [[Varuna]]] cannot be classed exclusively among the "gods of the sky" he nevertheless has qualities proper to the '''ouranian''' divinities. He is ''visva-darsata'', "everywhere visible", he "separated the two worlds", the wind is his breath; [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=P. Adams Sitney|chapter=The End of the 20th Century|title=Visionary Film: The American Avant-Garde 1943–2000|edition=3rd|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2002|page=417|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vlwU-MYE5pQC&pg=PA417|isbn=978-0-19-514885-5|passage=Conversely, {{w|Kenneth Anger}}'s only widely released film since 1972, ''[[w:Lucifer Rising (film)|Lucifer Rising]]'' (1980), uses a megalithic temple (not Stonehenge) and a number of ancient Egyptian sites in a [[w:Aleister Crowley|Crowleyan]] ritual hymn to chthonian and '''ouranian''' deities of power and light.}}
