#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Percy Bysshe Shelley|chapter=[[w:Symposium (Plato)|The Banquet]]. Translated from {{w|Plato}}.|editor=Mrs. Shelley [''i.e.'', {{w|Mary Shelley}}]|title=Essays, Letters from Abroad, Translations and Fragments, [...] In Two Volumes|volume=I|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Lea and Blanchard|year=a. 1822|year_published=1840|page=86|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_aIIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA86|oclc=1045107129|passage=For assuredly are there two Venuses; one, the eldest, the daughter of Uranus, born without a mother, whom we call the '''Uranian'''; the other younger, the daughter of Jupiter and Dione, whom we call the Pandemian;—of necessity must there also be two Loves, the '''Uranian''' and Pandemian companions of these goddesses. [...] [T]he attendant on the other, the '''Uranian''', whose nature is entirely masculine, is the Love who inspires us with affection, and exempts us from all wantonness and libertinism.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Steven Berg|chapter=Pausanias: Noble Lies and the Fulfillment of Greekness|title=Eros and the Intoxications of Enlightenment: On Plato’s Symposium|series=SUNY Series in Ancient Greek Philosophy|location=Albany, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:State University of New York|State University of New York Press]]|year=2010|section=part 1 (Athens and Enlightenment)|page=27|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KHjaGM7gYKAC&pg=PA27|isbn=978-1-4384-3017-1|passage=That this is indeed the general tendency of his &#91;{{w|Pausanias of Athens}}'&#93; speech is made clear by two remarks that follow immediately upon his differentiating the Pandemian from the '''Uranian''' gods. [...] But what is true for Aphrodite seems to be a contradiction in terms: she is both one and two and all gods must be praised—both '''Uranian''' and Pandemian—and yet only the '''Uranian''' ought to be praised [...].}}
# {{lb|en|not comparable|Greek mythology|dated}} Relating to [[Urania]], the [[Muse]] of [[astronomy]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Cyril Tourneur|title=The Transformed Metamorphosis|location=[London]|publisher=Printed by [[w:Valentine Simmes|Valentine Sims]],{{nb...|and are to be sold at the signe of the White Swan on Adling Hill}}|year=1600|oclc=1033539155|newversion=republished in|editor2=w:John Churton Collins|title2=The Plays and Poems of Cyril Tourneur: {{...|Edited with Critical Introduction and Notes by John Churton Collins}} In Two Volumes|location2=London|publisher2=[[w:Chatto & Windus|Chatto and Windus]],{{nb...|Piccadilly}}|year2=1878|volume2=II|page2=213|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=FhtaAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA213|oclc2=23159569|passage=Graced by nurses (art's nurse highly grac'd him) / Who fed him with pure marrow of the Muses; / And when he list, with moisture to refresh him, / He drunke cleare Helicon: cleare from abuses, / He bent his mind to pure '''Vranian''' vses, / Vranianie him did to heau'n vpreare: / And made to man, him demi-god appeare.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Vivek Iyer|title=Samlee’s Daughter: A Novel|chapter=8|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uDz9hJGr-HkC&pg=PT294|location=Great Britain|publisher=Polyglot Publications|year=2007|section=I|isbn=978-0-9550628-0-3|passage=Actually, this little girl-child should have been as happy as a lark. But, she wasn't. This is because her Bengali genes had her continually wish to be a Sarasvati, or '''Uranian''' Muse, to all the males who came within her ambit&nbsp;– incessantly inspiring them to great works of Art or Scientific discoveries.}}
